.TE
.PP
\fBsizeof\fR gives the size of a type or of an expression's type, in bytes.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    printf("%d %d %d %d\en", sizeof(i8), sizeof(i16), sizeof(i32), sizeof(i64));
    printf("%d %d %d\en", sizeof(f32), sizeof(f64), sizeof(bool));
    printf("%d %d %d\en", sizeof(u8 *), sizeof(u8[]), sizeof(String));
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1 2 4 8
4 8 1
8 16 24
.EE
.RE
.SH Integers
.PP
\fBi64\fR is what you use unless there is a reason not to: an integer literal is an \fBi64\fR, arithmetic on smaller integers is done in \fBi64\fR, and \fBprintf\fR\&'s \fB%d\fR prints all 64 bits.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    i64 population = 8100000000;
    i32 small = 2000000000;
    u8 byte = 200;
    printf("%d %d %d\en", population, small, byte);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
8100000000 2000000000 200
.EE
.RE
.SS Limits
.PP
The prelude, which every program sees without an import, names the smallest and largest value of each integer type.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    printf("i8:  %d .. %d\en", I8_MIN, I8_MAX);
    printf("i32: %d .. %d\en", I32_MIN, I32_MAX);
    printf("i64: %d .. %d\en", I64_MIN, I64_MAX);
    printf("u8:  0 .. %d\en", U8_MAX);
    printf("u32: 0 .. %u\en", U32_MAX);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
i8:  \-128 .. 127
i32: \-2147483648 .. 2147483647
i64: \-9223372036854775808 .. 9223372036854775807
u8:  0 .. 255
u32: 0 .. 4294967295
.EE
.RE
.SS Overflow wraps
.PP
Integer arithmetic wraps around at the type's size, as in C on every machine ktm runs on: there is no trap and no undefined behaviour to worry about. Division is the exception (below).
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    u8 b = 255;
    b = b + 1;
    i64 big = I64_MAX;
    big = big + 1;
    i8 small = 127;
    small = small + 1;
    printf("%d %d %d\en", b, big, small);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
0 \-9223372036854775808 \-128
.EE
.RE
.SS Division
.PP
Integer division rounds towards zero, and \fB%\fR takes the sign of the left side. Dividing an integer by zero (with \fB/\fR or \fB%\fR) stops the program with a crash report that says where it happened (see Safety and debugging (see ktm-guide-safety-and-debugging(7))). So does the one \fBi64\fR division whose result doesn't fit, \fBI64_MIN / \-1\fR (or \fBI64_MIN % \-1\fR): its report says \fBinteger overflow in division\fR\&.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    printf("%d %d\en", 7 / 2, \-7 / 2);
    printf("%d %d\en", 7 % 3, \-7 % 3);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
3 \-3
1 \-1
.EE
.RE
.SS Unsigned values
.PP
An unsigned type holds no sign. \fB%u\fR prints a value as unsigned; \fB%d\fR prints the same bits as signed.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    u64 all = U64_MAX;
    printf("%u\en", all);
    printf("%d\en", all);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
18446744073709551615
\-1
.EE
.RE
.PP
Arithmetic on integers smaller than \fBi64\fR is done in \fBi64\fR, so \fBx \- y\fR on two \fBu32\fR values below is -2. It wraps to the \fBu32\fR range only when it is stored in a \fBu32\fR\&. This differs from C, where the subtraction itself would wrap.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    u32 x = 3;
    u32 y = 5;
    printf("%d\en", x \- y);
    u32 diff = x \- y;
    printf("%u\en", diff);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
\-2
4294967294
.EE
.RE
.SH Floating point numbers
.PP
\fBf64\fR is a double, \fBf32\fR a single-precision float. A literal with a \fB\&.\fR or an exponent is an \fBf64\fR\&.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    f64 third = 1.0 / 3.0;
    f32 small = 1.0 / 3.0;
    printf("%.17f\en", third);
    printf("%.17f\en", small);
    printf("%f %.1f %e\en", 2.5, 1e6, 0.000123);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
0.33333333333333331
0.33333334326744080
2.500000 1000000.0 1.230000e\-04
.EE
.RE
.PP
Mixing an integer and a float gives a float. Dividing two integers stays an integer, so make one of them a float when you want the fraction.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    i64 total = 7;
    i64 count = 2;
    printf("%d\en", total / count);
    printf("%.1f\en", (f64)total / count);
    printf("%.1f\en", total / 2.0);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
3
3.5
3.5
.EE
.RE
.PP
Floats follow IEEE 754: dividing by zero gives an infinity, and a NaN compares unequal even to itself.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    f64 zero = 0.0;
    f64 inf = 1.0 / zero;
    f64 nan = zero / zero;
    printf("%f %f\en", inf, \-inf);
    printf("%d %d\en", nan == nan, inf > 1e308);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
inf \-inf
0 1
.EE
.RE
.SH bool
.PP
A \fBbool\fR is \fBtrue\fR (1) or \fBfalse\fR (0). Any value becomes a \fBbool\fR by being compared with 0, wherever the conversion happens: an assignment, an argument, a return, a cast.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
bool isSet(i64 flags, i64 bit) { return flags & bit; }

void main() {
    bool a = 0x100;          // true: the whole value counts, not its low byte
    bool b = (bool)0.5;      // true: not 0.0
    bool c = (bool)(\-1);     // true
    u8 *p = null;
    bool d = p;              // false: a null pointer
    printf("%d %d %d %d %d\en", a, b, c, d, isSet(0x800, 0x800));
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1 1 1 0 1
.EE
.RE
.PP
Conditions (\fBif\fR, \fBwhile\fR, \fB&&\fR, \fB!\fR) take any number or pointer the same way: non-zero is true.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    i64 count = 3;
    u8 *name = "ada";
    if (count && name) {
        printf("both set\en");
    }
    if (!0) {
        printf("!0 is true\en");
    }
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
both set
!0 is true
.EE
.RE
.SH null, true and false
.PP
\fBnull\fR is the pointer that points nowhere. It can be assigned to any pointer type, and a pointer compares equal to it when it is unset.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    u8 *name = null;
    if (name == null) {
        printf("no name yet\en");
    }
    name = "Ada";
    if (name) {
        printf("name: %s\en", name);
    }
    printf("%d %d\en", true, false);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
no name yet
name: Ada
1 0
.EE
.RE
.SH Converting between types
.SS Implicit conversions
.PP
A value converts to a wider type of the same kind without a cast: a smaller integer into a bigger one, an integer into a float, an \fBf32\fR into an \fBf64\fR, any pointer into \fBvoid *\fR\&.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    u8 b = 200;
    i64 wide = b;
    f64 f = wide;
    i32 neg = \-5;
    i64 alsoNeg = neg;
    printf("%d %.1f %d\en", wide, f, alsoNeg);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
200 200.0 \-5
.EE
.RE
.PP
A signed value keeps its sign when it gets wider (\fB\-5\fR stays \fB\-5\fR); an unsigned one is filled with zeros.
.SS Casts
.PP
\fB(type)value\fR converts explicitly. Going to a narrower integer keeps the low bits; going from a float to an integer drops the fraction.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    i64 big = 300;
    u8 low = (u8)big;
    f64 price = 9.99;
    i64 whole = (i64)price;
    i64 negWhole = (i64)\-2.7;
    printf("%d %d %d\en", low, whole, negWhole);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
44 9 \-2
.EE
.RE
.PP
A cast between pointer types changes how the same memory is read:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    u32 word = 0x04030201;
    u8 *bytes = (u8 *)&word;
    printf("%d %d %d %d\en", bytes[0], bytes[1], bytes[2], bytes[3]);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1 2 3 4
.EE
.RE
.PP
The bytes come out lowest first because every machine ktm runs on is little-endian.
.SH Writing numbers
.SS Integer literals
.PP
Decimal, hexadecimal (\fB0x\fR) or binary (\fB0b\fR). An \fB_\fR between two digits groups them, for reading.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    i64 dec = 42, hex = 0xFF, bin = 0b1010;
    i64 million = 1_000_000;
    i64 mask = 0xFF_FF;
    u32 flags = 0b1000_0001;
    printf("%d %d %d %d %d %d\en", dec, hex, bin, million, mask, flags);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
42 255 10 1000000 65535 129
.EE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
A literal too big for \fBi64\fR is a \fBu64\fR: \fB18446744073709551615\fR is \fBU64_MAX\fR\&.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB_\fR only goes between two digits: \fB_100\fR is a name, \fB1__0\fR and \fB1_\fR are errors.
.IP \(bu 2
There are no octal literals. A leading zero changes nothing: \fB0644\fR is six hundred and forty-four. ktm warns at such a literal and suggests writing it in hex.
.PP
A malformed number is an error that says what is wrong:
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    i64 x = 0b102;
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
error:
.EE
.RE
.SS Float literals
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    f64 a = 1.5, b = 1.5e3, c = 2e\-3, price = 1_000.25;
    printf("%.1f %.1f %.3f %.2f\en", a, b, c, price);
    printf("%s %s\en", typeof(2), typeof(2.0));
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
1.5 1500.0 0.002 1000.25
i64 f64
.EE
.RE
.SH Characters
.PP
A character constant is one character in single quotes. Its value is the character's code, so it can be used in arithmetic and compared.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    i64 a = 'A', newline = '\en', zero = '0';
    printf("%d %d %d\en", a, newline, zero);
    printf("%c%c%c\en", a, a + 1, a + 2);
    u8 c = 'x';
    printf("%c is a lower\-case letter: %d\en", c, c >= 'a' && c <= 'z');
    printf("'7' is the digit %d\en", '7' \- '0');
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
65 10 48
ABC
x is a lower\-case letter: 1
\&'7' is the digit 7
.EE
.RE
.PP
A character constant holds exactly one character (an escape counts as one). Two characters in quotes are an error: use a string, or an enum (see ktm-guide-enums(7)) for a set of tags.
.PP
This does not compile:
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    i64 c = 'ab';
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
error:
.EE
.RE
.SH Text
.PP
A string literal, in double quotes, is a \fBu8 *\fR to read-only bytes that end in a NUL (0). Wherever a slice is expected, it converts to a \fBu8[]\fR of the same bytes.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    u8 *greeting = "hello";
    u8[] view = "hello";
    printf("%s %s %d\en", greeting, view, view.len);
    printf("%d\en", sizeof("abc"));     // the three bytes and the NUL
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
hello hello 5
4
.EE
.RE
.PP
Two literals next to each other are joined into one, which helps with long text:
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    u8 *msg = "a long sentence written "
              "over two lines";
    printf("%s\en", msg);
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
a long sentence written over two lines
.EE
.RE
.SS Escapes
.PP
.TS
allbox tab(	);
lb lb
l lx .
T{
Escape
T}	T{
Means
T}
T{
\fB\en\fR \fB\et\fR \fB\er\fR \fB\eb\fR \fB\ef\fR \fB\ev\fR \fB\ea\fR
T}	T{
The C control characters
T}
T{
\fB\e\e\fR \fB\e'\fR \fB\e"\fR
T}	T{
A backslash, a quote
T}
T{
\fB\exHH\fR
T}	T{
A byte in hex, at most two digits: \fB"\ex01b"\fR is the byte 1, then \fBb\fR
T}
T{
\fB\e0\fR to \fB\e7\fR
T}	T{
An octal escape of up to three digits: \fB"\e033"\fR is ESC
T}
T{
\fB\ed\fR
T}	T{
A \fB$\fR
T}
.TE
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    printf("tab:[\et] quote:[\e"] backslash:[\e\e]\en");
    printf("hex:[\ex41\ex42] octal:[\e101] dollar:[\ed]\en");
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
tab:[	] quote:["] backslash:[\e]
hex:[AB] octal:[A] dollar:[$]
.EE
.RE
.PP
A string literal can't be changed: writing into one is an error when the compiler sees it, and a crash when it doesn't. Copy it into a buffer or a \fBString\fR first; Strings (see ktm-guide-strings(7)) shows how.
.SH Asking about a type
.PP
\fBtypeof(x)\fR is the name of an expression's type, as a string, made at compile time. \fBsizeof\fR and \fBalignof\fR give a size and an alignment in bytes.
.PP
.RS 4
.EX
void main() {
    u8 small = 1;
    i64 n = 5;
    printf("%s %s %s\en", typeof(small), typeof(small + 1), typeof("hi"));
    printf("%s %s\en", typeof(n * 2.0), typeof(&n));
    printf("%d %d %d\en", sizeof(small), sizeof(n), alignof(f64));
}
.EE
.RE
.PP
Output:
.RS 4
.EX
u8 i64 u8[3]
f64 i64 *
1 8 8
.EE
.RE
.PP
\fBsmall + 1\fR is an \fBi64\fR because arithmetic on integers smaller than \fBi64\fR is done in \fBi64\fR\&.
